Actually, once you understand how it differs from other RLs (which is to say:
drastically), Crawl has the easiest ID process of any roguelike.

Consumables: prepare (with only one potion and one scroll requiring more
preparation than "don't be on dangerous ground"[1])

Equipment: Steadfastly ignore equipment not relevant to your skills.
Wield-test the remaining weapons until you find something that makes further
IDing not worth the risk of a weapon of distortion (unless you can handle the
Abyss, of course), then wield-test only artifacts. (There may be artifacts of
distortion, but I've never seen one.) And just ignore absolutely all weapons
from then on. Continue wear-testing armor until you're blue in the face if
you want; if you limit your wear/wield-testing as mentioned above and, when
practical, wait to remove curses until there are multiple curses to remove,
you can get by on scrolls of remove curse alone, let alone scrolls of detect
curse.

Jewellery: Like armor, though many pieces don't auto-ID. Still, there are
more than enough scrolls of ID to handle these plus occasional non-jewellery
items.

Remaining: Mostly use-ID, with the occasional necessary or optional scroll-ID.

[1] Potions of poison (which show up early and require you to have a means of
healing or a decent amount of hitpoints) and scrolls of immolation (which
show up rather later and more or less require you to not be carrying other
scrolls when you read them). Well, there's strong poison as well, but the
likelihood of your not carrying healing and/or having poison resistance by
that point is small.

